# A small JEPA world model knows what happened, not what will

*Ravindu Somawansa — draft, 2026-11-01. Every number below is generated by `paper/make_paper.py` from `repro/*/expected/`; its source is in `paper/numbers.json`.*

## Abstract

We trained a LeWM-style JEPA world model (W1) on {{teleop_eps}} teleoperated demonstrations of one SO-101 tabletop task for ${{cost_w1}} and ran it through {{rows}} pre-registered uses taken from published world-model papers. Its encoder knows what happened: on {{n}} policy rollouts it never saw in training, a linear probe reads the outcome of a finished episode at AUROC {{rest_W1}}, level with a hand-built colour judge ({{rest_judge}}) and above a frozen DINOv2-S ({{rest_W2p}}), the commanded joints ({{rest_joints}}) and policy identity ({{rest_policy}}); at the grasp anchor the current latent reads the grasp outcome at {{anc_W1}} (DINOv2-S {{anc_W2p}}, joints {{anc_joints}}). It does not know what will happen: the imagined latent reads the grasp worse than the current one ({{c1_imagined}} vs {{c1_current}}), and ranking, progress, surprise monitoring, risk and multi-scale planning all miss their bars. One rig, one model, one data scale.

## 1. Introduction

World-model papers report the use that worked. We report every use we pre-registered for one small JEPA world model on one ${{arm_cost}} arm: {{rows}} gates, each with a bar, a dataset and a verdict, logged in a public register before or with the number ({{bar_first}} bars committed before their result, {{same_commit}} in the same commit). {{n_pass}} pass, {{n_met}} is met as predicted, {{n_np}} are not proven and {{n_fail}} fail or are no-go.

The result splits cleanly along the model. The *encoder* carries the episode's outcome, better than a frozen foundation encoder or the robot's own joint commands. The *predictor*, which every forecasting use relies on, does not turn that into a forecast. Contribution: (i) the register, with a load audit of every checkpoint use; (ii) controls that separate encoder from predictor; (iii) the reading that, at this data scale, the predictor is the weak link.

**Caveats stated up front.** The encoder readouts are linear probes fit with leave-one-session-out cross-validation on {{n}} rollouts ({{success}} success / {{failure}} failure, {{sessions}} sessions) with operator labels; W1 was trained on teleop only and never saw them, but the controls were chosen after the data and are **exploratory**. Two of the three passes have same-commit bars.

## 2. Setup

**Robot and data.** One SO-101 arm, two cameras (top, wrist), one task family (put a bowl in another). W1 is trained on `ravediamond/so101_bowls_all_trimmed` ({{train_eps}} train / {{val_eps}} validation episodes, split by grid layout); the evaluation rollouts are `ravediamond/so101-bowls-policy-rollouts`, a separate dataset of SmolVLA rollouts recorded after W1 was trained.

**Model.** W1 follows the LeWM recipe (multi-view ViT encoder, action-conditioned predictor, SIGReg), trained on one rented GPU. **Controls.** W2′: frozen DINOv2-S, mean-pooled, top + wrist. Joints: the commanded joint vector. G9c-3: a hand-built colour judge on the final frame (no learning). Policy identity: which checkpoint ran the episode.

**Load audit.** One early gate (G11-A) loaded W1 with `strict=False` and ran a random encoder. We therefore re-traced the load path of every use: {{audit_trained}} trained, {{audit_random}} random (G11-A, already voided), 0 unknown. The VLA-JEPA world-model block loads {{wm_keys}} / {{wm_keys}} keys ({{wm_missing}} missing).

## 3. The register

Table 1 lists the {{rows}} uses, grouped by what they need from the model.

*Encoder reads.* G10 step 1 reads the outcome at rest (pass, retrospective, same-commit); W1-C1's current-latent arm reads the grasp at the anchor ({{c1_current}}); the pose probes (LeJEPA) give W1 a mean R² of {{lejepa_w1}} against W2′'s {{lejepa_w2p}}, so the encoder is neither mainly kinematics nor the better pose reader.

*Predictor forecasts.* G0's open-loop prediction beats copy-last ({{g0_err}} vs {{g0_copy}} at K = 30): the predictor predicts its own latent. Every use of that prediction fails: G1b ranks chunks at top-1 {{g1b_top1}} (bar {{g1b_bar}}) while the commanded actions alone reach {{g1b_actions}}; W1-A's surprise catches {{w1a_hits}}; W1-C1's imagined latent ({{c1_imagined}}) is below the current one; SURVEYOR's τ is {{surv_tau_W1}} (paper {{surv_paper}}); HWM finds no crossover; G6a, G6b, W1-B, W1-C2, W1-R, REAP and G11-B-cal all miss their bars.

## 4. What the encoder knows

Table 2: outcome AUROC on the same {{n}} rollouts, leave-one-session-out, 95 % episode-bootstrap CIs.

| feature | at rest | grasp anchor ({{anc_scored}} scored, {{anc_fail}} fail) |
|---|---|---|
| W1 (ours) | {{rest_W1}} [{{rest_W1_lo}}, {{rest_W1_hi}}] | {{anc_W1}} |
| colour judge G9c-3 (no learning) | {{rest_judge}} [{{rest_judge_lo}}, {{rest_judge_hi}}] | — |
| W2′ frozen DINOv2-S | {{rest_W2p}} [{{rest_W2p_lo}}, {{rest_W2p_hi}}] | {{anc_W2p}} |
| commanded joints | {{rest_joints}} [{{rest_joints_lo}}, {{rest_joints_hi}}] | {{anc_joints}} |
| policy identity | {{rest_policy}} [{{rest_policy_lo}}, {{rest_policy_hi}}] | — |

At rest W1 is level with the colour judge (CIs overlap) and above the frozen encoder and the joints; at the grasp anchor, where no judge applies, it leads both controls. The original G10 gate scored W1 at {{g10_rest}} on a larger rollout set; the rerun here, on the {{n}} Orin rollouts with the controls beside it, gives {{rest_W1}}.

## 5. Where the predictor fails

The cleanest contrast is W1-C1: same encoder, same anchor, current latent {{c1_current}} vs imagined {{c1_imagined}} (bar {{c1_bar}} over current). Imagining forward loses outcome information the encoder had. In SURVEYOR, W1's latent-distance threshold fails both checks, while W2′ (τ {{surv_tau_W2p}}) and the joints (τ {{surv_tau_joints}}) pass the scale check S2: the predictor-facing geometry of W1 is the problem, not the task.

*Objection: G10 step 2 is a predictive pass.* The imagined latent beat policy identity by {{g10_anticip}} — but that bar is same-commit and retrospective, and the two bar-first reruns of the same idea (W1-C1, G11-B-cal) fail.

## 6. Limitations

Exploratory controls: {{n}} rollouts, operator labels, probes chosen after the data; fresh rollouts scored under a bar committed first are the test. One rig, one model, one data scale: a second scale point ({{scale_eps}} episodes) is costed and queued separately. Not run: W1-A surprise and W1-C1 imagined with W2′ / joints (each needs its own predictor), G6a T1 with the controls (needs a reimplementation). G0 and G10 bars are same-commit.

## 7. Conclusion

On a small teleop dataset, a JEPA world model learns an encoder that knows how the episode went and a predictor that cannot say how it will go. Reporting every pre-registered use, not the best one, is what makes that visible.
